hese two pixels are located ( 30 ) . Separability is achieved through a Kronecker product ( ⊗ \otimes ) of inter-channel (color) correlations and inter-pixel (spatial) correlations, with each component modeled as follows: – Inter-channel ( 3 × 3 3	imes 3 ) – The correlation between RGB channels is captured in full by the C ϕ ​ C ϕ ⊤ ∈ ℝ 3 × 3 C_{\phi}^{\vphantom{	op}}C_{\phi}^{	op}\in\mathbb{R}^{3	imes 3} term. – Inter-pixel ( d 2 × d 2 d^{2}	imes d^{2} ) – For the spatial component, we reason that natural images – seen as continuous functions of the infinite plane – have an approximately translation invariant distribution. Thus, their covariance operator has the Fourier modes as eigenfunctions ( 17 ) . For discretized (finite-size) images, the first practical parameterization that comes to mind is a diagonal matrix in the orthonormal basis given by the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) matrix. However, the DFT assumes cyclic boundary conditions which natural bounded images do not have – in other words, their covariance is not circulant (as a DFT-based model would assume) but rather Toeplitz ( 31 ) . In fact, empirically we find that the CIFAR-10 marginal covariance, in both the horizontal and vertical image directions, is the superposition of a Toeplitz component and a Hankel (“90-deg rotated Toeplitz”) component ( Figure 1 B). This suggests using the discrete cosine transform (DCT) instead: matrices diagonalized by the DCT have indeed been shown theoretically to possess precisely this Toeplitz + Hankel structure ( 36 ) . * Eigenbasis – We therefore consider a spatial covariance diagonalized by the 2-dimensional DCT operator F ⊗ F F\otimes F , which applies the standard 1-dimensional DCT operator F ∈ ℝ d × d F\in\mathbb{R}^{d	imes d} ( 39 ) to both the vertical and horizontal image dimensions (note that F − 1 = F ⊤ F^{-1}=F^{	op} ). * Eigenvalues – The D D eigenvalues are parameterized in positive real form as 𝝀 ϕ ​ ( 𝒙 t , t )  m{\lambda}_{\phi}( m{x}_{t},t) . Visu